 "is_vocab": false,
 "context": "<div data-type=\"note\" data-has-label=\"true\" id=\"fs-id1964835\" class=\"interactive ost-assessed-feature ost-tag-lo-apbio-ch14-s01-aplo-3-2\" data-label=\"Link to Learning\">\n<h3 class=\"os-title\" data-type=\"title\">\n  <span class=\"os-title-label\">Link to Learning</span>\n</h3>\n<div class=\"os-note-body\">\n<p id=\"fs-id2009505\">To see Miescher conduct an experiment step-by-step, click through <a href=\"https://openstax.org/l/miescher_levene\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\">this review</a> of how he discovered the key role of DNA and proteins in the nucleus.</p>\n<div data-type=\"injected-exercise\" data-injected-from-nickname=\"\" data-injected-from-version=\"13\" data-injected-from-url=\"https://exercises.openstax.org/api/exercises?q=tag:apbio-ch14-ex001\" data-tags=\"apbio-ch14-ex001 exid:apbio-ch14-ex001 context-cnxfeature:fs-id1964835\" data-is-vocab=\"false\" data-question-count=\"1\" data-is-multipart=\"False\">\n    \n    <div data-type=\"exercise-question\" data-is-answer-order-important=\"true\" data-formats=\"free-response multiple-choice\" data-id=\"145600\" id=\"145600\"><div class=\"os-problem-container\">\n<div data-type=\"exercise-context\" data-context-module=\"44bf14c6-0210-424d-9583-af663ce9f269\" data-context-feature=\"fs-id1964835\">Refer to <a class=\"autogenerated-content\" href=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-ap-courses/pages/14-1-historical-basis-of-modern-understanding#fs-id1964835\">[link]</a>\n</div>\n        <div data-type=\"question-stem\">Why were Phoebus Levene’s <a href=\"https://dnalc.cshl.edu/view/15672-Phoebus-Levene-c-1930-.html\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">discoveries</a> important to our current understanding of DNA?</div>\n        <ol data-type=\"question-answers\" type=\"a\">\n            <li data-type=\"question-answer\" data-id=\"555071\" data-correctness=\"\">\n                <div data-type=\"answer-content\">Phoebus Levene believed that the four nucleotides in DNA are not linked or repeated in the same pattern and that they are held together by phosphodiester bonds.</div>\n                \n            </li>\n            <li data-type=\"question-answer\" data-id=\"555070\" data-correctness=\"\">\n                <div data-type=\"answer-content\">He discovered that the nucleotides were held together by phosphodiester bonds, in which two phosphate groups bind two sugars together. This discovery led to our current understanding of DNA.</div>\n                \n            </li>\n            <li data-type=\"question-answer\" data-id=\"555069\" data-correctness=\"\">\n                <div data-type=\"answer-content\">He believed that proteins were less likely the vehicles for hereditary information. Later he discovered the four nucleotides in DNA which were linked together and repeated in a wide variety of different ways.</div>\n                \n            </li>\n            <li data-type=\"question-answer\" data-id=\"555072\" data-correctness=\"\">\n                <div data-type=\"answer-content\">He believed inaccurately that the four nucleotides in DNA repeated over in the same pattern. Also, he discovered that the nucleotides were held together by phosphodiester bonds in which the phosphate group binds two sugars together.</div>\n                \n            </li>\n            </ol>\n        </div></div>\n    </div>\n</div>\n</div>",
 "stimulus_html": "",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 233055,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Why were Phoebus Levene’s <a href=\"https://dnalc.cshl.edu/view/15672-Phoebus-Levene-c-1930-.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">discoveries</a> important to our current understanding of DNA?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 722365,
     "content_html": "Phoebus Levene believed that the four nucleotides in DNA are not linked or repeated in the same pattern and that they are held together by phosphodiester bonds."
    },
    {
     "id": 722364,
     "content_html": "He discovered that the nucleotides were held together by phosphodiester bonds, in which two phosphate groups bind two sugars together. This discovery led to our current understanding of DNA."
    },
    {
     "id": 722363,
     "content_html": "He believed that proteins were less likely the vehicles for hereditary information. Later he discovered the four nucleotides in DNA which were linked together and repeated in a wide variety of different ways."
    },
    {
     "id": 722366,
     "content_html": "He believed inaccurately that the four nucleotides in DNA repeated over in the same pattern. Also, he discovered that the nucleotides were held together by phosphodiester bonds in which the phosphate group binds two sugars together."
    }
   ],
